      "text": "We are given that\n\\[f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2 \\quad \\text{for all real numbers } x.\\]\nSetting $x = 3,$ we have\n\\[f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27.\\]Setting $x = -2,$ we have\n\\[f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12.\\]We first solve this system of equations for $f(-2)$ and $f(3).$  Multiplying the first equation by $2$ and subtracting the second equation, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n2f(3) + 4f(-2) - 2f(-2) - 4f(3) &= 54 - 12, \\\\\n-2f(3) + 2f(-2) &= 42, \\\\\n\\Rightarrow\\quad f(-2) &= 21 + f(3).\n\\end{align*}Substituting this into the equation $f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12,$ we find\n\\[21 + f(3) + 2f(3) = 12, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad 3f(3) = -9, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad f(3) = -3.\\]Therefore, $f(3) = \\boxed{-3}.$The answer is: -3",
