      "text": "To find \\( f(3) \\), we start with the given functional equation:\n\\[ f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2. \\]\n\nFirst, let's substitute \\( x = 3 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3(3)^2. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27. \\]\n\nNext, let's substitute \\( x = -2 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(1 - (-2)) = 3(-2)^2. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12. \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27 \\),\n2. \\( f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12 \\).\n\nWe can solve this system by elimination. First, let's label the equations for reference:\n\\[ (1) \\quad f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27, \\]\n\\[ (2) \\quad f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12. \\]\n\nTo eliminate \\( f(-2) \\), we can multiply the first equation by 2 and then subtract the second equation from it:\n\\[ 2(f(3) + 2f(-2)) = 2(27), \\]\n\\[ 2f(3) + 4f(-2) = 54. \\]\nNow subtract the second equation from this result:\n\\[ (2f(3) + 4f(-2)) - (f(-2) + 2f(3)) = 54 - 12, \\]\n\\[ 3f(-2) = 42, \\]\n\\[ f(-2) = 14. \\]\n\nNow that we have \\( f(-2) = 14 \\), we can substitute this value back into the first equation to find \\( f(3) \\):\n\\[ f(3) + 2(14) = 27, \\]\n\\[ f(3) + 28 = 27, \\]\n\\[ f(3) = 27 - 28, \\]\n\\[ f(3) = -1. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( f(3) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
